The dirt lane out of Drenfall shrinks to a footpath as you leave the last cottage behind. Chickens and gossip fade, replaced by the hush of wind through young birches. Ahead, the Whispering Forest Edge looms—trunks packed tight, their pale bark scarred with old lightning strikes.

Ten paces in, a rusted iron lockbox glints beneath a fallen pine. Its hinges are half-buried in last year’s needles. You step closer, boots crunching, when a shadow slips behind the log. No snap of twig, just a shift of darkness against moss. The box waits, lid cracked open an inch. Something keeps watch from the undergrowth.

