The box is warm against your palm, its weight almost nothing—like holding a bird’s wingbeat. A faint tremor passes through the wood, steady as a heartbeat. You lift it free of the brambles; no latch, no seam, just smooth panels that meet so perfectly you can’t feel where one ends and the next begins.

A twig-crunch sounds behind you, too deliberate to be wind. The shadow between two alders shifts, black on black, and the warm pulse in the box skips once, as if listening with you.

