--- MODEL OUTPUT ---
The box is heavier than you expect, its wood rough against your palm as you bolt through the market square. Shouts chase you—merchants, buyers, a few kids who think it’s a game. You duck past a fish stall, salt and ice crunching under your boots, and cut toward the treeline behind the inn.

A low growl rips the air. From the underbrush a Hollow Stalker lunges—matted fur, ribs showing, eyes clouded but fixed on you. Captain Oren’s voice cuts through the panic: “Focus. Don’t panic. Strike when it moves—not before.” The creature circles, claws scraping packed dirt.

You grip the box like a shield. When it springs, you swing hard; the corner cracks against its skull. It staggers. You kick, feel the jolt up your leg, then drive the box down again. The stalker collapses, breath rattling out. Silence returns, broken only by your pulse and the distant market din.

Oren jogs up, spear lowered. “First blood,” he says, nudging the carcass with his boot. “Carry that thing to the hedge-wall—burn it there.” He eyes the box. “And next time, ask before you borrow what isn’t yours.”

