You stand again in Old Well Square, boots scuffed from the road and Callum’s iron lockbox warm against your hip. Lanterns flicker on the timbered balconies above, throwing checkerboards of light across the cobbles. Callum pushes through the tavern door before you can knock, relief loosening the creases around his eyes.

He takes the box, snaps it open, and exhales as though he’s been holding his breath since you left. Without ceremony he presses a soft leather pouch into your palm; coins clink softly inside, heavy enough to feel useful. The satchel he drapes over your shoulder smells of old pipe-smoke and saddle-oil, its buckle worn smooth by someone else’s journeys. “No better way to thank you,” he mutters, already turning back inside, already safe.

