The weight of the coin pouch settles into your palm like a promise kept. Callum’s fingers, still smudged with ink, press the worn leather into your grip. The coins inside clink softly, each one stamped with Aurelion’s sun-burst crest.  

He steps back, shoulders easing as if a knot had loosened. “That lockbox held more than silver,” he says. “It held my good name. You’ve given both back to me.”  

The satchel’s strap is frayed but strong, smelling of cedar and old parchment. Whatever waits inside, it’s yours now—earned not by fate, but by the choice to help.

