--- MODEL OUTPUT ---
The pouch’s drawstring rasps against your wrist as you loosen it. Inside lie three copper coins, a brittle sprig of rosemary, and a thumb-sized vial of dew that catches the morning light like liquid glass. You tuck the pouch back under your cloak.

The old peddler lifts a gnarled hand in quiet reply, then turns to rearrange cracked pottery on his blanket. Dust puffs up from the road as you step past his stall and follow the wheel-ruts toward the crossroads.

Ahead, Callum swings open the lid of a wooden crate and sets out a neat row of tin cups. His stall is only three crates and a faded cloth, but the smell of fresh bread drifts from a covered basket. He notices you and smiles.

“Morning, traveler. Need a bite or a blade? I buy as readily as I sell.”

